Instruction: Read the following segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best reflects the provisions in the text.

Question: Is 'financial point of view' the sole consideration when determining whether an offer constitutes a Superior Proposal?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement: SECTION 8.2. Superior Offer Declaration. <omitted> 
(f) The Company Board may declare an offer a Superior Proposal only if all of the following conditions are met: 
    (i) The financial terms of the offer, while critical, must be evaluated in conjunction with nonfinancial strategic factors, including long-term corporate goals and market positioning; 
   (ii) In a process that involves detailed consultation with both external legal counsel and industry experts, the Board confirms that qualitative operational benefits, such as synergy potential and risk mitigation, are integral to the evaluation; 
  (iii) The Company is required to disclose to Parent a full spectrum analysis incorporating both financial and operational risks, ensuring that the decision is not based solely on numerical financial metrics; 
  (iv) Notwithstanding any prior references to financial performance benchmarks in other sections, the determination of a Superior Proposal explicitly mandates the integration of both quantitative financial analysis and qualitative strategic assessment; 
   (v) Should any subsequent offer present material changes, a reappraisal of both financial and nonfinancial factors is mandatory.

